[description]

This package provides an engine attachable to a [package map::display] widget, extending the map's
behaviour. The engine shows a set of [term geo/point] definitions taken from a store on the map,
as markers managed by [package canvas::edit::points] instances (used for display only, not for
editing).

It tracks the visible part of the map, ensuring that only the visible points use canvas resources
(items).

[para] The appearance of a point is determined by its [term style], selected by the
[const kind] attribute of the point. The engine predefines the styles

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const point]] A small circle.
[def [const feature]] A larger circle.
[def [const cluster]] A large circle labeled with the value of the point's [const count]
attribute, if it has one. [package map::point::store::memory] delivers clusters of points with
this kind and attribute.
[list_end]

Further styles are defined with the method [method add-style]. Every point shown must have a
[const kind] for which a style is defined.

[section {Class API}]

[list_begin definitions]
[call [cmd ::map::point::map-display] [arg objectName] [arg map] [arg store] [opt "[arg option] [arg value]..."]]
[call [cmd {::map point map-display}] [arg objectName] [arg map] [arg store] [opt "[arg option] [arg value]..."]]

This command creates a new engine named [arg objectName], attaches it to the [package map::display]
widget [arg map], and returns the fully qualified name of the object command. The name
[const %AUTO%] generates a unique name.

[para] The engine starts disabled, i.e. it shows nothing until its method [method enable] is
called.

[para] The [arg store] is a command prefix providing the points to show, with the API of
[package map::point::store::memory]. The engine uses its methods [method visible] to query the
points in the visible part of the map at the current zoom level, [method get] for their attributes
(of which it uses [const geo] and [const kind], passing the attributes other than [const names],
[const geo], and [const kind] to the style callbacks), and [method pixels] for their location on
the map, a [term point]. The store is invoked at the global level.

[para] The engine attaches to the map by chaining the option [option -on-view-change] of the
widget, see the section [emph {Attaching behaviours}] in the manpage of [package map::display].
[list_end]

[section Options]

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -on-active [arg command-prefix]]

A command prefix invoked at the global level, with the id of a point as additional argument,
whenever the mouse pointer enters the marker of that point. Leaving it is not reported.

The result of the command is ignored. The default is the empty string, i.e. no reporting.

This option can only be set at creation time.
[list_end]

[section Methods]

[list_begin definitions]
[call [arg objectName] [method add-style] [arg name] [opt "[arg option] [arg value]..."]]

This method defines the style [arg name], for the points whose [const kind] attribute is
[arg name]. An existing style can be redefined only while no point of that style has been shown
yet; otherwise an error is thrown.

The result of the method is the empty string.

[para] The options are those of [package canvas::edit::points] determining the appearance of the
markers, i.e. [option -color], [option -hilit-color], [option -radius], [option -kind], and
[option -create-cmd], plus the following pair of options, which have to be specified together:

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -hide-cmd [arg command-prefix]]
[opt_def -unhide-cmd [arg command-prefix]]

When these options are specified, the markers of points of this style leaving the visible part of
the map are not deleted, but hidden by invoking the [option -hide-cmd], and reused for other
points of the style. The [option -unhide-cmd] is invoked whenever a point of the style is placed
on the map, i.e. when it becomes visible and when the zoom level changes.

[para] Both are invoked at the global level with three additional arguments: the
[package canvas::edit::points] instance managing the markers of the style, the id of the marker
in that instance (which is also the canvas tag of its items), and the dictionary of the point's
attributes other than [const names], [const geo], and [const kind]. Their results are ignored.

[para] As the [option -create-cmd] does not get the attributes of the point, the
[option -unhide-cmd] is the place to adapt a marker to its point.
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method enable]]

This method enables the engine: it shows the points in the visible part of the map and from now
on follows changes of the visible part. Enabling an enabled engine does nothing.

The result of the method is the empty string.

[call [arg objectName] [method disable]]

This method disables the engine: it removes all points from the map and stops following the
changes of the visible part. Disabling a disabled engine does nothing.

The result of the method is the empty string.

[call [arg objectName] [method focus] [arg id]]

This method moves the map so that the point with the given [arg id] is at its center, at the
highest zoom level.

The result of the method is the empty string.

[call [arg objectName] [method destroy]]

This method disables the engine, detaches it from the map, and destroys it.
[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

Showing the points read from the [file *.points] files in directory [file points], with an
additional style for points of kind [const station]:

[example {
package require map::display
package require map::provider::osm
package require map::point::store::fs
package require map::point::store::memory
package require map::point::map-display

file mkdir tiles
set provider [map provider osm %AUTO% tiles]
map display .map -provider $provider -initial-geo {48.137 11.575} -initial-zoom 10
pack .map -fill both -expand 1

map point store fs     files  points
map point store memory store  files [expr {[.map levels] - 1}]
map point map-display  layer  .map store -on-active puts
layer add-style station -color blue -radius 5
layer enable
}]

